# Restart drain discipline

**Invariant:** `needed(restart) ⇒ fire(restart)` — busy is never a reason to skip one.
On `git_integration_worker`, busy means **drain** (never force, never kill). On every
other service, busy that defers a needed restart is cleared with `force=true`.

Operator bind 2026-07-31; MCP same-window classifier 2026-08-05; **force
generalized 2026-08-13**.

## Force is permitted — GIW is the sole exception (BINDING — operator 2026-08-13)

```
∀ service ≠ git_integration_worker:  force=true PERMITTED
service = git_integration_worker:    drain ∧ ¬force        # dispatch writes in progress
```

The discriminator is **what the interruption destroys**, not who owns the work:

| Interruption loses | Services | Force |
|---|---|---|
| An **attach handle** or a retryable request — reconnectable, nothing durable in flight | `mcp`, `cdp_ask`, `stargate`, `gateway`, `rag`, `cortex_api`, `event_service`, `cloud_proxy` | **Permitted** |
| An **in-progress durable write** — a dispatch mid-commit, truncated or lost | `git_integration_worker` | **Never** — arm the durable drain, exit, let it converge |

Apply this discriminator to any service added later; do not read the table as a
closed list. A seat facing `busy` on anything but GIW may force **without**
operator authorization, without a streaming/window test, and without waiting on
another seat. Latency is still not a *reason* to force, but it is no longer a
prohibition needing a justification — busy that blocks a needed restart is.

`busy=true` / `running_count>0` never licenses “park forever” or “skip restart.”
The live failure class is **abandonment**, not over-eager preemption: seats leave
fixes landed-not-live far more often than they wrongly interrupt a peer.

**Historical note:** the previous posture made force narrow across all services and
required a same-window/streaming classifier before forcing `mcp`. That classifier's
*recovery sequence* still binds (§ MCP); its *force-vs-wait gate* is retired.

## CSE vs service process (BINDING — 2026-08-12)

```
identity(CSE) = chat_url
claude.ai / Cowork is resilient to all fleet service restarts
restart(mcp | cdp_ask | …) ⇒ drop(attach) ∧ ¬end(CSE)
```

A Cowork CSE is a Chrome tab. Satellite `execution_id`, Playwright, and the MCP
socket are **attach handles**. Recycling those processes is reconnect, not
session-kill. Cowork is **not tightly coupled** to mcp, **cdp_ask**, or any other
fleet service — the tab survives; recover after `wait_healthy`. `cdp_ask`
`sync_restart` is **never coupled to mcp** (`api_dispatch._sync_restart`). After
`cdp_ask` recycle: `wait_healthy` → `project_ask(op=followup)` / `reattach=true`
by `chat_url` (tab should still be open). Continuity hop (new window, same
private lane) is for **MCP tooling/chip refresh**, not because a satellite row
died.

Another seat's mid-turn stream is a **courtesy** consideration, not a gate — since
2026-08-13 you may force through it. Prefer a short wait when the peer is visibly
mid-stream and your restart is not urgent; that is judgment, not permission. Do not
narrate `running_count` / `execution_id` gone as “the CSE is dead.” **Forbidden:** skip, park, or withhold
`mcp` **or** `cdp_ask` (or any other) `sync_restart` because it “would drop
Cowork attach.” Attach-loss is expected and recoverable.

## The wrong moves

| Wrong | Why | Right |
|---|---|---|
| `force=true` on **`git_integration_worker`** | Truncates a dispatch mid-write — the one irreversible loss | Arm the durable drain, report `restart_intent_id`, exit |
| "Busy / a dispatch or CSE is running, so I won't restart" | Leaves the fix landed-not-live; treats `cdp_ask` attach-loss as session-kill | Arm the restart. Force it if busy defers (¬GIW). After `cdp_ask`: recover the tab by `chat_url` |
| Asking the operator to authorize a force on a non-GIW service | Standing permission already covers it (2026-08-13); the ask is the latency | Force, announce, recover |
| "MCP/`cdp_ask` restart would drop Cowork attach / kill the CSE" | Conflates attach handle with CSE identity (`chat_url`); claude.ai is resilient to all service restarts | Restart (force if it defers). Recover: followup/reattach or continuity hop after healthy |
| Sit in-window polling `wait_healthy` while holding a lease | The lease you hold is what the drain waits on — never converges (friction 25989) | Arm, then **exit**; probe after |

## Mechanism already exists — use it, do not reinvent

`manage(action="sync_restart"|"restart"|"stop", service=…)` is drain-gated at the
shared chokepoint (`scripts/model_manager/ui/controller/restart_drain.py`), so both
MCP agents and the TUI get the same guard. Two shapes:

| Service | Behavior on busy |
|---|---|
| `git_integration_worker` | Durable **drain supervision** — worker stops accepting new mutating work, existing dispatches run to completion, SIGTERM only after `active_count == 0`. Returns `status=deferred, state=draining` with a `restart_intent_id`. Deadline is days, not seconds: drain is assumed inevitable |
| `mcp` | Busy-probe deferral — **not** NullBusyProbe. Defers when `cdp_ask` reports recorded project-ask work **or** MCP `/active-work` reports in-flight HTTP or recent life `tools/call` (TTL). Container SIGTERM HTTP drain alone does **not** protect Cowork between POSTs |
| `cdp_ask` | Busy when recorded `running_count > 0`; CDP attachment/session scalars are diagnostic only and never lifecycle leases. Product concurrency: open tabs may be many; only ≤3 may **stream** at once (`claude-ai-cdp-navigation` § Parallel Chrome) |
| everything else | Busy-probe deferral — `status=deferred, state ∈ {busy, in_progress, probe_error}` with `retry_after_s` |

Read state without touching the gate: `manage(action="busy_status")` — returns
`busy`, `restart_would_defer`, `active_work_summary` (one-line holder identity —
prefer it), and `restart_intent` per service. Safe to poll live.

## MCP / life-surface seat discipline (BINDING)

`mcp.busy=false` historically meant "no probe," not "Cowork idle." Before any
`sync_restart`/`restart`/`stop` of **mcp**, read `busy_status` (`mcp` + `cdp_ask`).
GIW code pickup does **not** require MCP restart. Descriptor / tooling MCP edits
follow the **same-window force protocol** below (operator bind 2026-08-05).

### MCP restart — recovery sequence (BINDING — 2026-08-05, force gate retired 2026-08-13)

`mcp` is **not** GIW: force it whenever busy defers. The window/streaming
classification that used to gate the force now only tells you **what recovery to
expect** afterward.

| Busy reason | Force | Expect afterward |
|---|---|---|
| **¬streaming** — `running_count=0`; idle CSE tabs are diagnostic only | Yes | Clean restart; CSE identity survives attach loss |
| Streaming ∧ **this** window (this seat's CSE / life connection) | Yes | **Your own MCP tool surface dies mid-stream.** After `wait_healthy(mcp)`: **continuity hop** (new CDP window, same private lane) — ¬ warm follow-up alone for tooling refresh |
| Streaming ∧ **another** window | Permitted | Peer's in-tab tools drop mid-turn; they recover by `chat_url`. Prefer a short wait if your restart is not urgent — courtesy, not a gate |

`open_attachment_count` ≠ streaming and does not gate restart. Observed CSE
pages without an active stream are attachment telemetry; `live_cse_count` may
deduplicate several page targets for one session. Open-tab count may be
arbitrary; the streaming concurrency bound (≤3 simultaneous; >3 untested)
lives in `claude-ai-cdp-navigation`.

Applies to IDE lead and operator-proxy (`cdp/opus` / web-anthropic on
`purpose=operator-proxy|mission`) alike. Operator-proxy often exercises this via
`contract: propagate`; cursor-auto auto-applies self-preempt force when
`allow_self_preempt` is true. **GIW is still exempt from all of it.**

Tooling / descriptor restart sequence: restart → **wait mcp healthy** →
**continuity hop** (`cdp-operator-proxy` Refresh ≠ follow-up). Commissioning
continuity **before** healthy is a defect.

Anti-patterns:
- treat `mcp.busy=false` + `in_flight=0` as proof Cowork life is idle
- treat an observed open attachment as a wait verdict when `running_count=0`
- withhold a force on `mcp` because a peer window is streaming (retired gate)
- skip `mcp` / `cdp_ask` / any service restart to “protect Cowork”; narrate `drop(attach)` as ending the CSE

## Procedure (binding)

1. **Probe before deciding** — `manage(action="busy_status")`. The only question the
   probe answers is **GIW or not**: GIW ⇒ arm the durable drain, never force;
   anything else ⇒ force is available. For `mcp`, read the recovery table above so
   you know what drops.
2. **Fire the restart.** On GIW, `status=deferred, state=draining` with a
   `restart_intent_id` **is** success. On every other service, a busy deferral is
   not an outcome to accept — re-fire with `force=true`. Do not park because
   an attachment is observed when `running_count` is zero, and do not route the
   force through the operator.
3. **Then wait, per seat:**
   - **Holding a write lease** (cursor-sdk / `cursor_sdk_gate`, or the dispatch
     whose work the drain is waiting on): the envelope's
     `caller_must_exit_to_release_lease` is binding — **exit now**, do not
     `wait_healthy` in-window. Your own exit is what lets the drain converge.
   - **IDE lead:** wait in the **background** (`presence-discipline` P4) — do not
     pin the turn. Poll `busy_status` / `wait_healthy` on a later turn, or let the
     `git_worker.drain.*` events land.
   - **cursor-auto:** report the armed `restart_intent_id` on the CLOSEOUT and
     finish. Do not block the dispatch on someone else's drain.
4. **Verify live before claiming live.** Landed ≠ live. Confirm the restart
   completed (process start time after the commit, or a live probe of the changed
   surface) before any claim that a fix is in effect.

## Live claim classes

`live` and `live@<sha>` are different claims. Ordinary `live` needs restart,
health, and a changed-surface probe. `live@<sha>` additionally needs a
path-explicit commit **before** restart, `code_ref_satisfied` (equal or ancestor),
identity movement, and relevant dirty-path disclosure. This ordering binds the
claim, not the restart: dirty-checkout restarts remain legal and are reported as
`live` plus tree state, never exact `live@<sha>`. Never withhold a needed restart
because the tree is dirty or a busy probe defers; downgrade the claim instead.

## `force=true` — permitted, one exception

```
force(service) PERMITTED  ⇔  service ≠ git_integration_worker
```

No operator authorization, no streaming test, no ownership proof. If a busy probe
defers a restart you need on `mcp`, `cdp_ask`, `stargate`, `gateway`, `rag`,
`cortex_api`, `event_service`, or `cloud_proxy`, re-fire with `force=true` and
announce it.

**`git_integration_worker` never.** Its drain exists because a forced stop can
truncate a dispatch mid-commit. That is the only loss in the fleet that no
reconnect repairs, which is why GIW gets durable drain supervision (days, not
seconds) instead of a force path. Arm it, put the `restart_intent_id` in your
closeout, exit so the drain can converge.

Still true: force is not a response to **latency**. Nothing about the standing
permission makes an impatient force correct when no restart is actually needed —
but once one is needed, busy is not a reason to withhold it, and asking permission
is not a substitute for firing it.

Killing a bridge or worker process directly (`pkill`, `kill`, `docker stop`) is
forbidden for lifecycle regardless of service — `services_ws.mdc` § Scripts.
`force=true` through `manage` is the sanctioned path; it is not process-killing.

